CAT = "caweldCommon"; SEG = "COM"
Q = [
("A welding machine is rated at 300 A with a 60% duty cycle. What does the 60% mean?",
 "6 minutes of every 10 at 300 A", ["60 minutes of every 100 at 300 A", "6 hours of every 10-hour shift", "60% of 300 A without overheating"],
 "Duty cycle is the share of a 10-minute period a machine can run at its rated output without overheating. At 60%, that is 6 minutes of arc time and 4 minutes of cooling."),
("Where should the work lead clamp be attached?",
 "On the work, close to the weld", ["On the building's steel frame", "On a nearby water pipe", "On the welding table's legs only"],
 "Clamping close to the joint keeps the welding current on a short, known path. Current wandering through structures, pipes or bearings can cause shocks, fires and damage."),
("You find a cracked insulator on an electrode holder. What should you do?",
 "Tag it out and replace it", ["Wrap it with electrical tape", "Use it with dry gloves only", "Use it at lower amperage"],
 "Damaged holder insulation exposes live metal and creates a shock hazard. Remove it from service and repair or replace it before use."),
("Before using a portable grinder, what must you check about the disc?",
 "Its rated speed is at least the tool's", ["Its diameter matches the guard opening", "It has been ring tested on the bench", "Its arbor hole is slightly oversize"],
 "A disc must be rated for at least the maximum speed of the grinder, or it can burst. Ring tests apply to vitrified bench wheels, not thin abrasive discs."),
("Which hazard is linked to grinding 2% thoriated tungsten electrodes?",
 "Radioactive dust", ["Phosgene gas", "Argon ignition", "Hexavalent chromium"],
 "Thorium oxide is mildly radioactive, so grinding dust should be captured with local exhaust or a dedicated grinder. Many shops now use ceriated or lanthanated tungsten instead."),
("How should a welding cable with a damaged jacket be repaired?",
 "Use an approved splice", ["Wrap it in duct tape", "Twist and tape the strands", "Leave it if no copper shows"],
 "Cables are repaired with approved insulated splices or connectors, or replaced. Tape repairs wear through quickly and leave a shock and fire hazard."),
("A two-leg sling lifts a 2000 kg load with each leg at 30° from the horizontal. What is the tension in each leg?",
 "2000 kg", ["1000 kg", "1155 kg", "4000 kg"],
 "Leg tension equals (load / number of legs) divided by the sine of the sling angle: 1000 / 0.5 = 2000 kg. At 60° it would be about 1155 kg, which is why low sling angles are dangerous."),
("During a crane lift, who may give the emergency stop signal?",
 "Anyone who sees a hazard", ["Only the designated signaller", "Only the crane supervisor", "Only the rigger who slung it"],
 "Normally only one designated signaller directs the operator, but the emergency stop signal must be obeyed from anyone."),
("Why should a tag line be attached to a suspended load?",
 "To control swing safely", ["To back up the main sling", "To lift the load evenly", "To ground the load"],
 "A tag line lets a worker steady and guide the load without standing under it or near pinch points. It is never a lifting component."),
("Before using a wire rope sling, which condition means it must be removed from service?",
 "A kinked or crushed section", ["Light surface rust", "A legible ID tag", "Grease between strands"],
 "Kinks, crushing, broken wires, bird-caging and heat damage are removal criteria. Light surface rust and lubrication are normal; a missing tag is also a reason to remove it."),
("What is the main hazard of heating metal that was cleaned with a chlorinated solvent?",
 "UV light can form phosgene gas", ["The solvent can cause arc blow", "The metal becomes magnetized", "It causes metal fume fever"],
 "Chlorinated solvent vapour exposed to arc UV or heat can break down into phosgene, a highly toxic gas. Keep degreasing away from welding areas and let parts dry fully."),
("Welding on galvanized steel without proper ventilation can cause:",
 "Metal fume fever", ["Arc eye", "Phosgene poisoning", "Carbon monoxide burns"],
 "Zinc oxide fumes cause metal fume fever, with flu-like chills and fever. Remove the coating where practical and use local exhaust or a respirator."),
("Which metal gives off hexavalent chromium fumes when welded?",
 "Stainless steel", ["Mild steel", "Aluminum", "Copper"],
 "Stainless steel contains chromium, and welding fume from it can contain hexavalent chromium, a carcinogen. Use local exhaust and respiratory protection."),
("A welder's eyes feel gritty and painful several hours after welding. What is the likely cause?",
 "Arc flash to the eye", ["A tungsten fragment", "Argon gas exposure", "Low oxygen in the area"],
 "Arc eye (photokeratitis) is a UV burn of the cornea. Symptoms typically appear hours after exposure, often from a neighbour's arc or lifting the helmet too early."),
("Before hot work on a drum that held a flammable liquid, what must be done?",
 "Clean it and purge or inert it", ["Open the bung and weld quickly", "Weld at the lowest amperage", "Fill it halfway with water"],
 "Even an empty drum can hold explosive vapour. It must be cleaned and then purged with inert gas or filled with water to just below the work, and tested before hot work."),
("Why must gas cylinders never be taken into a confined space during welding?",
 "Leaks can build up dangerous gas", ["They overheat from the arc", "They block the entry permit", "They interfere with the welding current"],
 "A leaking cylinder or hose can enrich or displace the atmosphere in a small space. Keep cylinders outside and test the atmosphere before and during entry."),
("What document lists the essential variables for making a specific weld?",
 "Welding procedure", ["Bill of materials", "Material test report", "Hot work permit"],
 "A welding procedure specification, or the welding procedure data sheet under CSA W47.1, sets the process, filler, position, preheat, current range and other variables the welder must follow."),
("Which CSA standard covers certification of companies that fusion weld steel?",
 "CSA W47.1", ["CSA W59", "CSA W48", "CSA Z94.3"],
 "CSA W47.1 sets out company certification, including approved procedures and qualified welders. CSA W59 covers welded steel construction, W48 covers filler metals and Z94.3 eye and face protection."),
("The procedure calls for a preheat you cannot reach with the heating equipment on site. What should you do?",
 "Stop and ask the supervisor", ["Weld and note it afterwards", "Use the closest preheat you can", "Raise the welding current"],
 "Essential variables on the procedure must be met. If one cannot be, stop and get direction from the welding supervisor or engineer rather than changing it yourself."),
("A drawing note says to weld to CSA W59. What does that standard cover?",
 "Welded steel construction", ["Electrode storage only", "Respirator selection", "Pressure vessel inspection"],
 "CSA W59 is the Canadian standard for welded steel construction, including design, workmanship and inspection of welds in steel structures."),
("What is the main purpose of preheating a carbon steel joint before welding?",
 "Slow cooling to prevent cracks", ["Burn off the mill scale", "Raise the tensile strength", "Speed up electrode melting"],
 "Preheat slows cooling, which reduces hardening in the heat-affected zone and lets hydrogen escape, so cracking is less likely. Thicker plate and higher carbon steel usually need more preheat."),
("How is a temperature-indicating crayon used to check preheat?",
 "Its mark melts at its rating", ["Its colour fades as it cools", "It glows when too hot", "It shows the exact temperature"],
 "A temperature-indicating crayon is rated for one temperature: its mark melts when the steel reaches it. Use one rated at the minimum preheat and another at the maximum interpass temperature."),
("Which steel would normally need the most preheat?",
 "Thick, high carbon equivalent", ["Thin, low carbon steel", "Thin plate in the flat", "Thick, low carbon steel"],
 "Hardenability rises with carbon equivalent and cooling speed rises with thickness, so thick, high carbon equivalent steel is most prone to cracking without preheat."),
("Interpass temperature is measured:",
 "Just before the next pass", ["Only before the root pass", "Only after the final pass", "One day after welding"],
 "The interpass temperature is the temperature of the weld area just before the next pass. Procedures set a minimum, usually the preheat, and often a maximum."),
("Which nondestructive test finds surface cracks in austenitic stainless steel?",
 "Liquid penetrant testing", ["Magnetic particle testing", "Hardness testing", "Guided bend testing"],
 "Austenitic stainless steel is essentially non-magnetic, so magnetic particle testing will not work. Liquid penetrant reveals surface-breaking flaws on any non-porous metal."),
("Which test can show porosity and slag deep inside a groove weld?",
 "Radiographic testing", ["Visual testing", "Liquid penetrant testing", "Magnetic particle testing"],
 "Radiography (and ultrasonic testing) can find internal flaws. Visual and penetrant tests only find surface flaws, and magnetic particle testing finds surface and near-surface flaws."),
("What does a guided bend test check on a qualification coupon?",
 "Weld soundness and ductility", ["Base metal hardness", "Exact tensile strength", "Filler chemistry"],
 "The coupon is bent around a set radius; cracks or open flaws on the stretched surface reveal lack of fusion, porosity or brittleness."),
("Why do some codes delay the final visual inspection of high strength steel welds?",
 "Hydrogen cracks form later", ["Slag shows better cold", "Spatter must oxidize", "Paint must cure first"],
 "Hydrogen-induced cracking can form hours after the weld cools, so inspection may be delayed to catch it."),
("What is the purpose of peening a weld bead?",
 "To reduce residual stress", ["To raise the bead's height", "To remove porosity", "To add filler metal"],
 "Peening stretches the bead to offset shrinkage stress and distortion. It is usually not done on root and cap passes and only where the procedure permits it."),
("What does post-weld heat treatment mainly do?",
 "Relieves stress", ["Removes slag inclusions", "Fills undercut", "Cleans the weld surface"],
 "Post-weld heat treatment heats the weldment to a set temperature and cools it slowly, relieving residual stress and tempering hard zones."),
("What is the safest way to make an electrode holder safe when stopping work?",
 "Remove the rod, hang it up", ["Lay it on the steel work", "Leave a rod in it on the bench", "Drop it in a pail of water"],
 "Remove the electrode and place the holder where it cannot touch the work or a grounded object. Switch the machine off at the end of the job."),
("Before starting a job, which step comes first when planning the work?",
 "Review the drawings", ["Strike a test arc on scrap", "Order more filler metal", "Tack the parts together"],
 "Planning starts with the drawings, specifications and procedure, which set materials, sequence, process and inspection requirements."),
]
